    {
      "@id": "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/SC-16",
      "@type": "Scenario",
      "name": "Cross-VTC Credential Recognition — Ahmed Joins a Second VTC",
      "description": "Ahmed holds a valid VRC from Laila's VTC and applies to join a second affiliated VTC. The second VTC's admission policy accepts one cross-VTC VRC as a substitute for one of its required two VRCs, provided the issuing VTC is on its trust registry. The receiving VTA must resolve the issuing VTC's C-DID, verify its governance framework version, retrieve the dependency map for Ahmed's VRC, and confirm no cascade revocation has occurred. If Ahmed's first-VTC membership is later revoked, the second VTC must be notified via the dependency map mechanism.",
      "lifecyclePhase": "Cross-VTC Interoperability",
      "legitimate_personas": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/D3",
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/D1"
      ],
      "governance_decisions": "1. What is the minimum dependency record a cross-VTC credential must carry to enable the receiving VTA to verify its continued validity?\n2. At what point does cross-VTC revocation propagate — immediately, at next presentation, or on a scheduled reconciliation?\n3. Is registry-anchored delegation (IDVP authority chain) the correct model for cross-VTC credential recognition, or does it require a new pattern?\n4. Does the dependency map need to be signed by the issuing VTC, the receiving VTC, or both?",
      "userStories": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/US-16"
      ],
      "metrics": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/M-17",
