    {
      "@id": "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/SC-17",
      "@type": "Scenario",
      "name": "Governance Amendment — Daniel Updates the Constitution Mid-Lifecycle",
      "description": "After a governance vote, Daniel amends the VTC admission threshold from two VRCs to three. Version 1.1 of the governance framework is published with a defined effective date. Ceremonies that reached AGREED state before the effective date are bound to version 1.0 and must complete under those rules. New ceremonies initiated after the effective date apply version 1.1. The VTA's PEP must enforce version binding at ceremony AGREED state. A member who was mid-ceremony at the effective date must not have their in-progress ceremony invalidated by the amendment.",
      "lifecyclePhase": "Governance Amendment / Lifecycle",
      "legitimate_personas": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/D1",
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/D5"
      ],
      "governance_decisions": "1. Is governance version binding at PROPOSED, AGREED, or IN_PROGRESS state?\n2. What is the data model for a constitution version reference in a ceremony profile?\n3. How does the VTA determine which governance version to apply when a ceremony spans the effective date of an amendment?\n4. What is the remediation path if the VTA applies the wrong version to a live ceremony?",
      "userStories": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/US-17"
      ],
      "metrics": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/M-25",
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/M-16",
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/M-19"
      ],
      "provenance": {
        "source": "workbook:UserStories_v6.xlsx",
        "imported": "2026-08-05"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/SC-18",
      "@type": "Scenario",
      "name": "Adjudication — Independent Review of a Disputed Anchor Denial",
      "description": "Laila denies Ahmed's VRC application. Ahmed believes the denial is discriminatory and initiates a formal adjudication request. An independent governance body — not Laila, not Daniel, not any party to the original ceremony — is appointed as adjudicator. The adjudicator reviews the ceremony profile, the denial failure record, Ahmed's terms declaration, and Laila's registry query evidence. The adjudicator issues a binding ruling artefact. The VTA is required to honour the ruling. If the ruling overturns the denial, Laila must re-enter the ceremony or a substitute anchor must be appointed.",
      "lifecyclePhase": "Adjudication / Appeals",
      "legitimate_personas": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/D3",
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/D2",
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/D6"
      ],
      "adversarial_personas": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/B2"
      ],
      "governance_decisions": "1. What authorises the adjudicator to issue a binding ruling — what credential does the adjudicator hold, and who issued it?\n2. Is adjudication a new interaction pattern or a constrained subtype of Restore?\n3. What is the data model for a binding ruling artefact, and what trust task produces it?\n4. Can a state actor coerce the adjudicator body, and what structural mitigations exist?\n5. If the ruling requires Laila to re-enter a ceremony she has declined, what consent mechanism applies?",
      "userStories": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/US-18"
      ],
      "metrics": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/M-26",
