    {
      "@id": "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/SC-22",
      "@type": "Scenario",
      "name": "Pre-Condition Violation — Exchange Initiated Without Prior Identify",
      "description": "A developer integrating a VTC client application makes an implementation error: their code sends a ceremony PROPOSED message before completing DID resolution for the counterparty. The counterparty's M-DID is unresolved. The ceremony proceeds to PROPOSED state. At Level D, the signature check fails. The scenario tests whether the VTA's PEP enforces the Identify-before-Exchange pre-condition at the protocol layer (producing a standard pre-condition violation artefact) or whether the failure manifests as an implementation-specific runtime error with no interoperable representation.",
      "lifecyclePhase": "Pre-condition Enforcement / Security",
      "legitimate_personas": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/D6"
      ],
      "governance_decisions": "1. Is Identify-before-Exchange a mandatory protocol pre-condition enforced by the PEP, or a recommended implementation practice?\n2. What is the standard data model for a pre-condition violation artefact — is it a subtype of failure record?\n3. Does a pre-condition violation produce ceremony state FAILED or a new state (INVALID/REJECTED)?\n4. Is the pre-condition violation artefact visible only to the initiating party, or is it shared with the counterparty and community log?\n5. How does the spec distinguish a pre-condition violation (implementation error) from a deliberate identity spoofing attempt (adversarial)?",
      "userStories": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/US-22"
      ],
      "metrics": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/M-15",
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/M-19",
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/M-16"
      ],
      "provenance": {
        "source": "workbook:UserStories_v6.xlsx",
        "imported": "2026-08-05"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/SC-23",
      "@type": "Scenario",
      "name": "Endorsement as Primary Pattern — Professional Reputation in the Open-Source VTC",
      "description": "Daniel's VTC is a community of open-source maintainers. Members endorse each other's contributions in specific domains (e.g., cryptography, governance design, accessibility). Laila endorses Ahmed for \"decentralised identity protocol design\" after collaborating on a proposal. Six months later, Laila retracts the endorsement — not because Ahmed did anything wrong, but because a later collaboration revealed a significant disagreement about approach. The retraction is prospective: it does not invalidate past decisions made on the basis of the endorsement, but Ahmed's current reputation profile no longer includes it. A recruiter queries the endorsement graph and the topology is verifiable for anomalous clustering.",
      "lifecyclePhase": "Endorsement / Reputation",
      "legitimate_personas": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/D2",
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/D3"
      ],
      "adversarial_personas": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/B1"
      ],
      "governance_decisions": "1. Is endorsement a subtype of Attest, or does it require its own trust task type with different revocation semantics?\n2. What is the data model distinction between endorsement retraction (prospective) and credential revocation (retrospective)?\n3. What is the data model for an endorsement record, and how does it differ from a VRC?\n4. Is the endorsement graph topology a verifiable claim (signed by the VTA) or an application-layer analytics output?\n5. How does the accumulation pattern apply to endorsements — what is the aggregation rule and decay function?",
      "userStories": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/US-23"
      ],
      "metrics": [
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/M-03",
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/M-05",
        "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/M-21",
